\f1\fs22 Tony was attempting to write lyrics about evolutionary biology when his tablet rang, displaying a number that somehow managed to look pretentious. Only one person he knew could make digits seem condescending.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Johnny," Tony guessed, already regretting it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Tony! My favorite fairy-boxer philanthropist!" The Johnny's voice oozed through the speaker like designer oil. "How's the billionaire lifestyle? Drowning in money and existential ennui yet?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm trying to work."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Work? You? That's adorable. Like watching a fish attempt calculus." The Johnny paused, presumably to admire himself in something reflective. "Listen, I have a proposition. The kind that changes the world and makes us both legends. Well, makes me more of a legend. You'd be legend-adjacent."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony stared at his empty notepad, where he'd written "Evolution is..." forty-seven times without managing to finish the sentence. The High Elvis expected new material for the Rock Against Revival festival. So far, Tony had rhymed "selection" with "erection" and given up. "I'm listening."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Of course you are. People always listen to me. It's the burden of being fascinating." The sound of expensive ice in an expensive glass tinkled through the connection. "I need Gyges Discs. Three of them. Hidden in stasis units in abandoned Airborne mansions. Very hush-hush. Very important. Very much the sort of thing that requires someone with your... connections."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"My connections?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Elvi worship you like you're Darwin's gift to sequins. One word from their Great Teacher and they'd steal the moon if it helped you write better lyrics."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony's pencil snapped. He'd been holding it too tight. "You want me to ask the Elvi to commit burglary?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I prefer 'liberation of scientific resources.' Has a revolutionary ring to it. Very evolutionary. See what I did there?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"No."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Evolution, revolution\'97it's wordplay. The kind of sophisticated humor you'd understand if you hadn't spent your formative years below the Layer." The Johnny sighed dramatically. "Look, it's simple. The Elvi break into some empty houses, grab the refrigerators, extract the discs. No one gets hurt, science advances, I become even more magnificent. Everyone wins."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Why would I do this?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Because you need something from me." The Johnny's voice took on that special quality reserved for people who thought they were clever. "Something small, insignificant, barely worth mentioning. Access to Sarah."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony's heart performed a drum solo that would have made the Elvi weep. "Sarah's at Sublime Point. It's locked down tighter than\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Than your girlfriend's legs? Yes, I know. Hilarious how she married that walking haircut Alexander. Though I suppose when you're carrying the prophesied child of universal rebirth, you can't be picky about fathers." He paused. "Oh wait, that's not the father either, is it? Poor Tony. Always the cleaner, never the cleaned."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Get to the point before I hang up and block your number."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You can't block excellence, Tony. It finds a way." More ice tinkling. "Here's the deal: you get me three Gyges Discs, I get you into Sublime Point. A few days with Sarah and her miracle womb. Quality time with Little Clinton, the Amazing Prophecy Baby. Maybe she'll even let you hold her hand. You'd like that, wouldn't you? Physical contact with someone who doesn't immediately need antibiotics?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony found himself standing without remembering getting up. "How?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm Project Manager for the Gyges Initiative. Capital letters and everything. I have access. More importantly, I have Sarah's work schedule. Tuesday through Thursday, she's in the lab. Perfect time for a conjugal visit. Well, not conjugal. That ship has sailed, hit an iceberg, and become a James Cameron film. But you could be in the same room. Breathe the same air. Watch her not love you in high definition."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You're an asshole."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm an asshole with a key card. Very different." The Johnny's smugness was practically audible. "Besides, don't you need to be near the baby to write? Something about mystical inspiration and maternal proximity? The Elvi are expecting a concert that'll save their souls from gospel corruption. Hard to deliver that from your tower of lonely luxury."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony hated that he was right. Since leaving Sublime Point, his creativity had evaporated like dignity at a corporate team-building exercise. The High Elvis had already "collected" him once. Next time might involve less kidnapping and more permanent disappointment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Three discs," Tony said slowly. "And you'll get me in to see Sarah?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Scout's honor. Though I was expelled from Scouts. Apparently, 'maverick innovation' isn't a valid merit badge." The Johnny cleared his throat. "Do we have a deal?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I need to write. The festival is in days and I've got nothing. If I can't be near Little Clinton\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Yes, yes, the baby is your muse, you're artistically constipated, it's all very tragic. Do we have a deal?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony looked out his window at the Ghetto, where the Elvi were probably doing something ridiculous and meaningful. "Fine. But if you screw me over\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You'll what? Write a mean song about me? I'm trembling." The Johnny laughed, a sound like champagne bubbles developing personality disorders. "Have your sequined friends meet me tomorrow. Caf\'e9 Quantum in Mount Victoria. Try to look less... yourself. We're aiming for subtle."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Elvi don't do subtle."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Then tell them it's a new kind of subtle. With more rhinestones." The connection ended with a click that somehow conveyed both victory and hair gel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony stared at his tablet, then at his empty notepad, then at the window where the Ghetto glittered like a disco ball having an identity crisis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Three discs. That's all. Just convince the Elvi to commit some light burglary for the chance to maybe see Sarah. The woman who'd fled his apartment like he was contagious. Who'd looked at him like he was something that crawled out of a stasis unit gone wrong.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But she was carrying Little Clinton. And Little Clinton was the key to everything\'97the Elvi's future, Tony's lyrics, possibly the universe's continued existence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He picked up his tablet to call the High Elvis. This was going to require significant explanation and possibly choreography.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Evolution is..." he muttered, then added: "...apparently criminal."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It wasn't his best work, but it was a start.\

